WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
With mean arterial pressure held steady, each tissue sets its own flow by its

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.280
arterioles. But who turns that tap?

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.620
And what does it listen to?

4
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:18.327
When a tissue works harder, its arterioles relax and flow follows.

5
00:00:19.587 --> 00:00:22.407
No single signal does it; several overlap.

6
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:30.147
From the working tissue come adenosine, K+, CO2, H+ and low O2.

7
00:00:31.407 --> 00:00:34.747
The vessel lining, the endothelium, adds nitric oxide.

8
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:37.797
The same tap does a second job.

9
00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:43.637
It can hold flow steady when perfusion pressure changes, while the tissue's demand

10
00:00:43.637 --> 00:00:47.277
stays the same. This is autoregulation.

11
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:51.357
Plot pressure along the bottom, flow up the side.

12
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.057
At low pressures, flow rises with pressure.

13
00:00:56.597 --> 00:01:00.717
Across a middle range, the line runs nearly level, though not perfectly flat.

14
00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:04.257
Above that range, flow rises again.

15
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:09.117
Remember: flow equals pressure drop divided by resistance.

16
00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:14.097
Across that middle range, pressure climbs but flow barely moves.

17
00:01:15.397 --> 00:01:17.257
So what must the arterioles be doing?

18
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:29.823
is Narrowing. For flow to stay put while pressure rises, resistance must rise in

19
00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:33.163
proportion. When pressure falls, they widen.

20
00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:40.063
One mechanism is the myogenic response: a small artery reacting to the pressure

21
00:01:40.063 --> 00:01:41.063
across its wall.

22
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.473
In the heart, flow tracks oxygen use.

23
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:50.333
In 1963, Robert Berne made cat and dog hearts short of oxygen.

24
00:01:51.773 --> 00:01:56.473
Coronary resistance fell, and the muscle released breakdown products of adenosine.

25
00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:03.723
He proposed that adenosine forms as ATP breaks down when oxygen supply falls short.

26
00:02:04.963 --> 00:02:07.783
It diffuses to the arterioles and dilates them.

27
00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:12.483
Later blockade studies in dogs found it a potent dilator.

28
00:02:13.623 --> 00:02:17.803
But its role is not evident unless oxygen delivery is compromised.

29
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:20.497
Here is the one idea.

30
00:02:21.817 --> 00:02:24.277
In every organ, the arterioles are the tap.

31
00:02:25.457 --> 00:02:28.437
What changes from organ to organ is who turns it.

32
00:02:29.417 --> 00:02:31.757
In the heart, metabolism turns it.

33
00:02:32.957 --> 00:02:35.657
In the brain, active neurons raise local flow.

34
00:02:36.897 --> 00:02:42.537
Carbon dioxide matters too: a rise dilates brain vessels, and a fall constricts

35
00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:43.477
them.

36
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.360
In working muscle, metabolites turn it.

37
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.320
In human studies, no single dilator explains much more than 20 to 30 percent of the

38
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:58.000
rise. Working muscle also blunts sympathetic constriction.

39
00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:01.063
In skin, nerves turn it.

40
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:06.443
Skin flow follows body temperature more than the skin's own metabolism.

41
00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:12.703
In human whole-body heating, it has been estimated to reach 6 to 8 L per min.

42
00:03:13.817 --> 00:03:15.557
The tap is the same everywhere.

43
00:03:16.777 --> 00:03:21.977
The hand on it is not: pressure, the tissue's own chemistry, neurons, or nerves

44
00:03:21.977 --> 00:03:23.077
tuned to body heat.
